[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Abstract Classes and Interfaces in OOP"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Object-Oriented Programming relies heavily on abstraction to manage complexity by hiding unnecessary details and showing only essential features. Two of the most powerful tools for achieving abstraction in modern programming languages are Abstract Classes and Interfaces."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is an Abstract Class?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An abstract class is a restricted class that cannot be used to create objects directly. To use it, it must be inherited from another class. Abstract classes can contain both abstract methods (methods without a body) and regular methods (methods with a body)."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cannot be instantiated using the 'new' keyword."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Can have constructor methods, member variables, and implemented methods."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Subclasses must implement all inherited abstract methods unless the subclass itself is also abstract."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"abstract class Animal {
    String name;

    public Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    // Abstract method (no body)
    public abstract void makeNoise();

    // Regular method
    public void sleep() {
        System.out.println(name + " is sleeping...");
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"What is an Interface?"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"An interface is a completely "abstract class" that is used to group related methods with empty bodies. It defines a contract that implementing classes must follow."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"All methods in an interface are public and abstract by default (prior to Java 8 default methods)."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Attributes are implicitly public, static, and final."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Supports multiple inheritance, allowing a class to implement multiple interfaces."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"interface Flyable {
    int MAX_SPEED = 300;
    void fly();
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences: Abstract Class vs. Interface"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Inheritance: A class can extend only one abstract class, but it can implement multiple interfaces."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"State: Abstract classes can have instance variables and state; interfaces cannot (only static final constants)."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Design Philosophy: Abstract classes represent an 'IS-A' relationship and code reuse. Interfaces represent a 'CAN-DO' capability or behavior contract."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choose abstract classes when you want to share code among closely related classes. Choose interfaces when you want to define common behaviors for unrelated classes or enforce a strict API contract across your architecture."}]
